#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992} :activity/id #uuid "eedb9663-ba7e-5ff2-ad61-597482d1636e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944 :topic/id #uuid "73f31c1a-1d34-559e-8292-a932c5c83421" :topic/math-academy-id 934} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093402 :tutorial/content "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39b17ee8-086a-5282-a414-c88d455b6107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bdb49ae-e07e-5e55-b869-25f67ee0ac21" :step/math-academy-id 16467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092023 :question/id #uuid "cb7d8dc0-98eb-53d0-9e48-3b1c2d7f6308" :question/math-academy-id "e-4508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394786}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "7e787e4f-33b0-514d-ad4f-569e9dfb525d" :step/math-academy-id 16468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092024 :question/id #uuid "422d31ad-8caa-5ce2-a0e0-c905fcc2640d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393215}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"} :step/id #uuid "696d1030-ccb5-5376-b23e-62d38e346070" :step/math-academy-id 16469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093404 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=3 \\\\ 6x+3y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we use the matrix method of $\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ to find a solution to this system?\n\nThis system can be represented in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo use this matrix method, we need to find $A^{-1}.$ However\n\n$$\n\\det (A) = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 6 =0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is not invertible. So we cannot use the matrix method to find a solution to the system.\n\nIn general, if the coefficient matrix $A$ is not invertible, there is no unique solution. This means that either\n\n- a solution may not exist, or\n\n- there may be infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5e2bff7-df5b-5628-92e3-a50ca8dd6be8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3066 :tutorial/title "Systems of Equations Where the Coefficient Matrix is Singular"} :step/id #uuid "ff58cfed-22a0-501b-8983-7edc00d85964" :step/math-academy-id 16614}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092433}]}
